<LessonLayout title="4.3 — Pydantic: Structured Data for Agents">

  <LessonHeader
    num="4.3"
    title="Pydantic: Structured Data for Agents"
    subtitle="Turning an LLM's free-text answers into validated, typed data that the next step can rely on."
  />

  <div class="lesson-content">

    <LessonToc />

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">The Problem: Text Is Not Data <SourceBadge source="course" /></h2>

    <p>
      An LLM's natural output is a string. That's fine for conversation, but not for building
      applications. Ask it to extract a user's name and order number from an email, and you might get
      any of these:
    </p>

    <CodeBlock lang="text" label="three correct answers" code={inconsistent} />

    <p>
      All correct, all differently shaped. A downstream program expecting one format breaks on the
      others. Agents need <strong>controlled, standardized output</strong>, and the common way to get it
      is to ask for JSON and check it with <strong>Pydantic</strong>.
    </p>

    <LessonDiagram definition={`
flowchart LR
  subgraph A["Without a schema"]
    direction TB
    L1["🤖 LLM"]:::llm --> T1["Alex's order is 8675309"]:::warn
    L1 --> T2["Order: 8675309, Name: Alex"]:::warn
    T1 --> X["💥 downstream code<br/>can't rely on the shape"]:::warn
    T2 --> X
  end
  subgraph B["With a Pydantic model"]
    direction TB
    L2["🤖 LLM + schema<br/>in the prompt"]:::llm --> J["JSON"]:::out --> P{"🚦 Pydantic<br/>validate"}:::gate
    P -->|"valid"| O["✅ Order object<br/>.name, .order_id"]:::done
    P -->|"invalid"| R["🔁 retry with<br/>the error"]:::retry
  end

  classDef llm   fill:#dbeafe,color:#1e3a8a,stroke:#2563eb,stroke-width:2px
  classDef out   fill:#fef9c3,color:#713f12,stroke:#ca8a04,stroke-width:2px
  classDef gate  fill:#ede9fe,color:#4c1d95,stroke:#7c3aed,stroke-width:2px
  classDef done  fill:#dcfce7,color:#14532d,stroke:#16a34a,stroke-width:2px
  classDef warn  fill:#fee2e2,color:#7f1d1d,stroke:#dc2626,stroke-width:2px
  classDef retry fill:#ffedd5,color:#7c2d12,stroke:#ea580c,stroke-width:2px
    `} />

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">What a Pydantic Model Is <SourceBadge source="course" /></h2>

    <p>
      A <strong>blueprint (schema) for your data</strong>: a Python class that inherits from
      <code>BaseModel</code> and lists each field you expect, its type (<code>str</code>,
      <code>int</code>, <code>bool</code>, …), and whether it's required or optional.
    </p>

    <CodeBlock lang="python" label="user_profile.py" code={userProfile} />

    <div class="lesson-table">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>You write</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><code>user_id: int</code></td><td>The field's name and type</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>Field(..., description=…)</code></td><td><strong>Required</strong>: the <code>...</code> (Python's Ellipsis) means "no default value"</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>Optional[str] = Field(None, …)</code></td><td><strong>Optional</strong>: may be missing or null; defaults to <code>None</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>description="…"</code></td><td>Documentation that ends up in the schema, so the LLM reads it too</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">What Pydantic Does For You <SourceBadge source="course" /></h2>

    <h3 class="lesson-heading">1. Validation</h3>

    <p>
      Incoming data (like the LLM's JSON) is checked against the model. Wrong types, missing required
      fields, or malformed JSON raise a <strong>clear error</strong>, which is exactly what a gate check
      needs.
    </p>

    <CodeBlock lang="python" label="validation-error" code={errorDemo} />

    <p>
      The error names <em>every</em> problem and its field, readable by you and by the LLM, which is why
      it makes good retry feedback.
    </p>

    <h3 class="lesson-heading">2. Parsing</h3>

    <p>Valid data becomes a clean Python object you access with dot notation:</p>

    <CodeBlock lang="python" label="parsing" code={parseDemo} />

    <LessonCallout variant="warn" title="Two behaviors that surprise people">
      <p>
        Pydantic quietly converts types (<code>"42"</code> → <code>42</code>), and
        <code>Optional</code> doesn't mean "can be left out". Both are explained with examples in
        the next section.
      </p>
    </LessonCallout>

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">Two Gotchas <SourceBadge source="mine" /></h2>

    <p>
      Both come from Pydantic doing something different from what the code <em>looks like</em> it
      says. Every result below comes from running it on Pydantic 2.13.
    </p>

    <h3 class="lesson-heading">Gotcha 1: Optional[str] doesn't mean "can be left out"</h3>

    <p>
      In Python type hints, <code>Optional[str]</code> means "a string <strong>or None</strong>": it
      describes which <em>values</em> are allowed. It says nothing about whether the key can be
      <strong>missing</strong>. Pydantic decides that by one thing only: <strong>does the field have a
      default?</strong>
    </p>

    <CodeBlock lang="python" label="optional_trap.py" code={optionalTrap} />

    <div class="lesson-table">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Declaration</th><th>Key missing</th><th><code>null</code></th><th><code>"a@b.com"</code></th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><code>email: str</code></td><td>❌ Field required</td><td>❌ must be a string</td><td>✅</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>email: Optional[str]</code></td><td>❌ <strong>Field required</strong></td><td>✅ None</td><td>✅</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>email: Optional[str] = None</code></td><td>✅ None</td><td>✅ None</td><td>✅</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>email: str = "unknown"</code></td><td>✅ "unknown"</td><td>❌ must be a string</td><td>✅</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <LessonDiagram definition={`
flowchart LR
  Q1{"Does the field<br/>have a default?"}:::q
  Q1 -->|"no"| R["Required:<br/>key must be present"]:::warn
  Q1 -->|"yes"| O["Can be left out:<br/>gets the default"]:::done
  Q2{"Is None in the type?<br/>Optional[...]"}:::q
  Q2 -->|"no"| N1["null rejected"]:::warn
  Q2 -->|"yes"| N2["null accepted"]:::done

  classDef q    fill:#ede9fe,color:#4c1d95,stroke:#7c3aed,stroke-width:2px
  classDef warn fill:#fee2e2,color:#7f1d1d,stroke:#dc2626,stroke-width:2px
  classDef done fill:#dcfce7,color:#14532d,stroke:#16a34a,stroke-width:2px
    `} />

    <p>
      <strong>Why it bites with LLMs:</strong> when there's no email in the text, the most natural thing
      for a model to do is <em>leave the key out</em>. With <code>Optional[str]</code> alone, that
      perfectly reasonable answer fails the gate check.
    </p>

    <LessonCallout variant="key" title="Remember">
      <p>
        <code>Optional[...]</code> controls which <strong>values</strong> are allowed;
        <code>= default</code> controls whether the field can be <strong>left out</strong>. For a truly
        optional field you usually want both: <code>Optional[str] = None</code>.
      </p>
    </LessonCallout>

    <h3 class="lesson-heading">Gotcha 2: lax mode quietly converts types</h3>

    <p>
      By default Pydantic runs in <strong>lax mode</strong>: if a value has the wrong type but can
      reasonably be converted, it converts it instead of raising an error.
    </p>

    <CodeBlock lang="python" label="lax_coercion.py" code={laxDemo} />

    <div class="lesson-table">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Field type</th><th>LLM sent</th><th>Lax (default)</th><th>Strict</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td rowspan="5"><code>bool</code></td><td><code>"yes"</code>, <code>"true"</code>, <code>"on"</code>, <code>"y"</code>, <code>"1"</code>, <code>1</code></td><td>✅ True</td><td>❌ error</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>"no"</code>, <code>"false"</code>, <code>"off"</code>, <code>"0"</code>, <code>0</code></td><td>✅ False</td><td>❌ error</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>"maybe"</code>, <code>"probably"</code></td><td>❌ error</td><td>❌ error</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>2</code></td><td>❌ error</td><td>❌ error</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>true</code> (real JSON boolean)</td><td>✅ True</td><td>✅ True</td></tr>
          <tr><td rowspan="3"><code>int</code></td><td><code>"42"</code>, <code>" 42 "</code>, <code>"42.0"</code>, <code>42.0</code></td><td>✅ 42</td><td>❌ error</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>42.5</code>, <code>"42.5"</code></td><td>❌ error (would lose data)</td><td>❌ error</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>"forty-two"</code></td><td>❌ error</td><td>❌ error</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p>
      Often that's helpful, since LLMs regularly put numbers in quotes. <strong>The pitfall is the
      signal you lose.</strong> If you asked for a JSON boolean and got <code>"yes"</code>, the model
      didn't follow your format, and the next answer might be <code>"probably"</code>. Because the
      conversion is silent:
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>The gate passes</strong>, so you never learn the model is drifting from the schema.</li>
      <li><strong>You trust Pydantic's fixed rules</strong> for what the text meant, which may not be what you'd decide.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>To require exact types, use <strong>strict mode</strong>:</p>

    <CodeBlock lang="python" label="strict_mode.py" code={strictDemo} />

    <p>
      Now the mismatch raises a <code>ValidationError</code>, and its message becomes retry feedback
      ("is_active should be a valid boolean"), so the model corrects the format instead of the problem
      being hidden.
    </p>

    <div class="lesson-table">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Mode</th><th>Use when</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Lax (default)</td><td>You mainly want a usable value, and small format slips like quoted numbers don't matter</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Strict</td><td>The exact format matters, or you want to catch the model ignoring the schema early, e.g. claim triage (4.4), where a silently converted value can send a claim to the wrong queue</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p>
      Strict mode can also be set per field, e.g. <code>is_active: bool = Field(strict=True)</code>, so
      you can be strict where it matters and lax elsewhere.
    </p>

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">Two Uses in an Agent Workflow <SourceBadge source="course" /></h2>

    <p>One model, used twice: it <strong>describes</strong> what you want, then <strong>checks</strong> what you got.</p>

    <LessonDiagram definition={`
flowchart LR
  M["📐 UserProfile<br/>Pydantic model"]:::model
  M -->|"model_json_schema()"| PR["📝 Prompt<br/>respond with JSON<br/>matching this schema"]:::step
  PR --> L["🤖 LLM"]:::llm --> RAW(["raw JSON text"]):::out
  RAW --> G{"🚦 model_validate_json()<br/>gate check"}:::gate
  M -.->|"same model"| G
  G -->|"✅ valid"| N["➡️ next step gets<br/>a typed object"]:::done
  G -->|"❌ ValidationError"| F["🔁 retry, error text<br/>as feedback"]:::retry

  classDef model fill:#ede9fe,color:#4c1d95,stroke:#7c3aed,stroke-width:2px
  classDef step  fill:#f1f5f9,color:#0f172a,stroke:#64748b,stroke-width:2px
  classDef llm   fill:#dbeafe,color:#1e3a8a,stroke:#2563eb,stroke-width:2px
  classDef out   fill:#fef9c3,color:#713f12,stroke:#ca8a04,stroke-width:2px
  classDef gate  fill:#ede9fe,color:#4c1d95,stroke:#7c3aed,stroke-width:2px
  classDef done  fill:#dcfce7,color:#14532d,stroke:#16a34a,stroke-width:2px
  classDef retry fill:#ffedd5,color:#7c2d12,stroke:#ea580c,stroke-width:2px
    `} />

    <div class="lesson-table">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Use</th><th>What it does</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>In the prompt</td><td>The schema (field names, types, descriptions) goes in as context, telling the LLM the exact JSON structure to produce</td></tr>
          <tr><td>In the gate check</td><td>The same model parses and validates the response before it moves to the next step</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <CodeBlock lang="python" label="prompt_and_gate.py" code={promptAndGate} />

    <LessonCollapsible title="What does model_json_schema() produce?" label="Output">
      <p>The model, converted to JSON Schema: this is what the LLM sees in the prompt (abridged):</p>
      <CodeBlock lang="json" label="UserProfile.model_json_schema()" code={schemaOut} />
      <p>
        Your <code>description</code> strings and the <code>required</code> list come straight from the
        class, so writing good descriptions is prompt engineering (3.1) and documentation at once.
      </p>
    </LessonCollapsible>

    <LessonCallout variant="info" title="You've used this already">
      <p>
        The research gate in 4.2 (<code>Finding(**item)</code>) is this same pattern. Pydantic checks
        the <strong>shape</strong>; whether the content is true (do the quotes exist in the source?)
        still needs its own check.
      </p>
    </LessonCallout>

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">Exercise: An Order Model <SourceBadge source="course" /></h2>

    <p>
      An agent processes e-commerce orders. To keep agents, databases, and application code consistent,
      define two models:
    </p>

    <div class="lesson-table">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Model</th><th>Field</th><th>Type</th><th>Required?</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td rowspan="4"><code>Order</code></td><td><code>order_id</code></td><td>int</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>customer_email</code></td><td>str</td><td>No</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>items</code></td><td>List of <code>OrderItem</code> (one or more)</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>total_amount</code></td><td>float</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
          <tr><td rowspan="3"><code>OrderItem</code></td><td><code>sku</code></td><td>str</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>quantity</code></td><td>int</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>item_name</code></td><td>str</td><td>No</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <LessonDiagram definition={`
classDiagram
  class Order {
    int order_id
    Optional~str~ customer_email
    List~OrderItem~ items
    float total_amount
  }
  class OrderItem {
    str sku
    int quantity
    Optional~str~ item_name
  }
  Order "1" --> "1..*" OrderItem : items
    `} />

    <LessonCollapsible title="Show solution" label="Solution">
      <CodeBlock lang="python" label="order_models.py" code={exerciseSolution} />
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Define <code>OrderItem</code> first</strong>, since <code>Order</code> refers to it.</li>
        <li><strong>Nesting is just a type:</strong> <code>List[OrderItem]</code> makes Pydantic validate every item with the <code>OrderItem</code> model.</li>
        <li><strong>"One or more":</strong> <code>min_length=1</code> enforces it. Without it, <code>items: List[OrderItem]</code> would accept an empty list.</li>
        <li><strong>Optional fields need a default:</strong> <code>= None</code>. <code>Optional[str]</code> alone means "may be null" but is still required in Pydantic v2.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Errors point to the exact spot, even inside nested lists:</p>
      <CodeBlock lang="python" label="nested errors" code={exerciseErrors} />
    </LessonCollapsible>

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">Beyond the Course: Instructor and Structured Outputs <SourceBadge source="mine" /></h2>

    <p>
      <strong>Instructor</strong> isn't an alternative to Pydantic; it's built on it. Pydantic knows
      nothing about LLMs: it defines and validates data. Instructor wraps the LLM call around a Pydantic
      model and automates everything in the diagram above: sending the schema, parsing the reply, and
      retrying with the validation errors.
    </p>

    <LessonDiagram definition={`
flowchart TB
  subgraph Y["What you write with plain Pydantic (this lesson, 4.2)"]
    direction LR
    a1["schema → prompt"]:::you --> a2["call the LLM"]:::you --> a3["model_validate_json"]:::you --> a4["retry loop with<br/>error feedback"]:::you
  end
  subgraph I["What Instructor does for you"]
    direction LR
    b1["client.create(response_model=User,<br/>max_retries=3)"]:::lib --> b2["✅ validated User object"]:::done
  end
  Y -. "same steps, automated" .-> I

  classDef you  fill:#fef9c3,color:#713f12,stroke:#ca8a04,stroke-width:2px
  classDef lib  fill:#dbeafe,color:#1e3a8a,stroke:#2563eb,stroke-width:2px
  classDef done fill:#dcfce7,color:#14532d,stroke:#16a34a,stroke-width:2px
    `} />

    <CodeBlock lang="python" label="instructor_example.py (from the Instructor docs)" code={instructorDemo} />

    <div class="lesson-table">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th></th><th>Pydantic + your own code</th><th>Instructor</th><th>Provider structured outputs</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>What it is</td><td>Data validation library</td><td>LLM client wrapper built on Pydantic</td><td>An API feature: the model is constrained to your JSON schema while generating</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Retries with error feedback</td><td>You write the loop</td><td>Built in</td><td>Rarely needed for shape; still needed for content rules</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Works across providers</td><td>Yes (you call the API)</td><td>Yes, one interface for OpenAI, Anthropic, Google, …</td><td>Per provider, each with its own API and schema limits</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Custom content rules (validators)</td><td>Yes, and you handle failures</td><td>Yes, failures are fed back to the model automatically</td><td>No; you validate afterwards</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Best for</td><td>Learning; full control; unusual flows</td><td>Production extraction with retries, especially across providers</td><td>Guaranteed shape from one provider</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p>
      <strong>When to use which:</strong> write it yourself while learning (you'll understand what the
      libraries automate) or when you need a non-standard flow. Use Instructor once you're doing
      structured extraction in production and don't want to maintain retry and parsing code. Either way,
      Pydantic models remain the source of truth, and everything above still applies.
    </p>

    <LessonCallout variant="key" title="Shape is guaranteed; truth isn't">
      <p>
        No matter which tool produces the object, a valid <code>UserProfile</code> only means the JSON
        had the right shape. Content checks (4.2), like quotes existing in the source or values being
        plausible, are still your job, though a Pydantic <em>validator</em> can encode many of them so
        they run on every parse.
      </p>
    </LessonCallout>

